{% set local_problem %}
<p>Here's what happens to sectional gutters on Lake Guntersville:</p>

<p>Standard sectional gutters come in 10-foot sections joined with slip connectors and sealant. Four sections to cover 40 feet means three joints that have to stay watertight. In a normal environment — say, Huntsville — that sealant lasts 12-15 years before needing attention.</p>

<p>On the lake? Cut that in half. The humidity here runs 75-85% year-round. Morning fog rolls off 69,000 acres of water and settles on everything. That moisture gets into seams and stays there. The sealant cycles between wet and dry constantly, breaking down the bond. By year 6-8, you've got seams that weep. By year 10-12, they're actively leaking onto your fascia.</p>

<p>We measured a job on <a href="/recent-projects/">North Lake Drive</a> where the homeowner thought his gutters were "getting old." They were 9 years old. The aluminum at the seams had corroded thin enough to see light through. The fascia behind three different joints had rot spreading 8-10 inches in each direction. That's what lake humidity does to sectional gutters.</p>

<p>The <a href="/recent-projects/">Point of Pines</a> and <a href="/recent-projects/">Brown's Creek</a> areas get hit especially hard — north shore exposure means more wind-driven moisture. The <a href="/recent-projects/">south shore</a> is slightly more protected but the humidity still does its work.</p>

<p>Seamless gutters solve this by eliminating the problem. No seams along the runs means no seams to fail. The only joints are at corners and downspout connections — and those are welded or sealed differently than section-to-section joints.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Seamless gutters are fabricated from continuous aluminum coil stock — one piece, custom-cut to the exact length of each run on your home. Instead of 10-foot sections joined together, your 47'8" gutter is actually 47'8" of unbroken material.</p>

<p>We bring a portable gutter machine to your property — basically a rolling mill on a trailer. Flat aluminum coil goes in one end, formed gutter profile comes out the other. We measure to the quarter-inch, dial in the length, and run it. What comes out is ready to install.</p>

<p>For Guntersville lake properties, we use .032 gauge aluminum instead of standard .027. That's 18% thicker metal. More rigid, more resistant to denting from debris and ladders, and significantly more corrosion resistance over time. The cost difference is about $1-2 per linear foot — maybe $200-300 on a typical house. In this environment, it's worth it.</p>

<p>Quick truth about "seamless": there are still seams at corners and downspout outlets. But on a simple house, that might be 8-10 joints total instead of 20-25 with sectional. Fewer joints means fewer failure points. In lake humidity, that translates directly to longer life.</p>
{% endset %}

